Asking Claude...
Based on the Slack messages in the #engineering channel, **Bob** was the team member who connected the conflicting user reports to the actual technical implementation.

**Evidence Bob provided:**

On January 18, 2025 at 10:30:00, Bob made the critical connection by stating:

> "@alice Wait a minute... @diana that 30/70 split in engagement metrics... that exactly matches our feature flag distribution! Could we be seeing the difference between new and old search algorithms?"

**What Bob identified:**

1. **The pattern match**: Bob noticed that Diana's analytics showing a 30/70 split in user engagement metrics exactly matched the 30/70 feature flag distribution for the search algorithm rollout.

2. **The root cause**: He realized that the "conflicting user reports" (some saying search was amazing, others saying it was broken) weren't bugs at all - they were two different user groups experiencing two completely different search algorithms due to the A/B test.

3. **The implementation detail**: Bob had deployed the feature flag service himself on January 12th that automatically bucketed users into 30% new ML algorithm and 70% old algorithm based on a consistent hash of user IDs.

Diana confirmed Bob's insight immediately afterward, and this led the team to realize that even their own team members (Alice and Charlie) were in different test cohorts, which explained why they had been seeing different search behaviors during internal testing.

